1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,870
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,030
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:22,610
Today we're tackling something that often trips people up, English idioms, specifically the ones you hear all the time in business.

6
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:23,590
It's so true.

7
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:28,420
You could have perfect grammar, great vocabulary, but then someone says, you know, let's touch base.

8
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:31,800
And it throws you off in the workplace.

9
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:33,060
It's full of them.

10
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:34,020
Absolutely.

11
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:39,890
So we've dug into a source that really focuses on these common phrases native speakers use, especially at work.

12
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:43,010
Think of this as, well, a practical guide.

13
00:00:43,310 --> 00:00:49,040
Yeah, a way to help you understand what people really mean and maybe feel a bit more confident using these yourself.

14
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:49,730
Exactly.

15
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:54,950
Our mission is to unpack these idioms, look at their meaning, see how they're used with examples from our source.

16
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:55,670
Why bother?

17
00:00:56,170 --> 00:00:58,980
Well, like our source says, even fluent speakers can struggle.

18
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:04,420
And mastering these is key to, you know, sounding natural and getting the full meaning in business talk.

19
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,690
That jump from literal words to the figurative meaning.

20
00:01:08,830 --> 00:01:09,550
That's the tricky part.

21
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:10,910
Our source really highlights this.

22
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:13,210
You know, you hear, give me a ballpark figure.

23
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:13,630
Right.

24
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:14,890
And they're not talking about baseball.

25
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:16,170
They just want an estimate.

26
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:16,860
Precisely.

27
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:21,010
Or instead of let's start this project, it's almost always.

28
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:22,130
Let's get the ball rolling.

29
00:01:22,170 --> 00:01:22,370
Yeah.

30
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:26,110
Or waiting for approval becomes waiting for the green light.

31
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,320
So these are the kinds of phrases we're going to break down.

32
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:31,080
Okay, let's do it.

33
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:37,830
Let's start with idioms around getting things started, kicking off projects.

34
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:42,090
First up, the one we just mentioned, to get the ball rolling.

35
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:46,390
Simple enough, it just means to start something, get a process or project going.

36
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:49,290
Often implies you're starting it so others will join in.

37
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:54,180
Our source had examples like, let's schedule a meeting to get the ball rolling on the new project launch.

38
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:55,140
Makes sense.

39
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:59,080
Or, once we secure the funding, we can get the ball rolling on the construction.

40
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:00,840
It's all about that first push.

41
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:04,470
And often, before you can roll any balls, you need permission, right?

42
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:06,570
That brings us to get the green light.

43
00:02:06,590 --> 00:02:07,990
Yeah, exactly like a traffic signal.

44
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:09,830
It means you've got approval, you've got permission to start.

45
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:10,390
Go ahead.

46
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:15,810
So examples like the marketing campaign received the green light from the client and now we can launch it.

47
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:21,210
Or maybe the pharma company finally got the green light to sell their new drug.

48
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:25,350
You'll also just hear people say we're waiting for the green light from the CEO.

49
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:27,140
It's that go ahead signal.

50
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:31,600
But OK, even with the green light, sometimes things are fuzzy.

51
00:02:31,590 --> 00:02:32,610
Plans aren't set.

52
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:35,310
Our source calls this up in the air.

53
00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:35,850
Right.

54
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:41,160
It means undecided, uncertain, not yet determined, just floating.

55
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:42,500
You hear it all the time.

56
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:47,720
Our plans are up in the air or our vacation plans are still up in the air because we can't decide where to go.

57
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:53,370
Yeah, it can be about specifics like many details are still up in the air or just everything.

58
00:02:53,430 --> 00:02:53,730
I don't know.

59
00:02:53,770 --> 00:02:55,130
Everything is up in the air right now.

60
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:56,510
Total uncertainty.

61
00:02:56,590 --> 00:02:59,660
And when things are up in the air, you can't really have a detailed plan.

62
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,560
So you might need to play it by ear.

63
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:03,740
Yeah, like improvising.

64
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:04,960
You make decisions as you go.

65
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,730
You see how things develop and then decide what to do next.

66
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:08,950
No fixed plan.

67
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:12,210
The source had examples like, we don't have specific travel plans.

68
00:03:12,230 --> 00:03:13,350
We're just playing it by ear.

69
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:14,350
That sounds familiar.

70
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:16,070
Or more businesslike.

71
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:21,280
Since the situation is constantly changing, we'll have to play it by ear and adjust our strategy accordingly.

72
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:23,140
Even in meetings.

73
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:24,120
I'm not sure what I'll say.

74
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:24,960
I'll just play it by ear.

75
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:26,620
It's all about being flexible.

76
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:32,410
OK, so starting, getting approval, dealing with uncertainty, check.

77
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:34,590
But work isn't always smooth.

78
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:36,120
What about problems?

79
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:40,240
There's that classic one, especially for meetings, the elephant in the room.

80
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:41,560
Oh, yeah, that's a great idiom.

81
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:47,950
It's that big, obvious issue or problem that everyone knows is there, but nobody wants to talk about it.

82
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:49,530
Because it's awkward.

83
00:03:49,510 --> 00:03:50,680
Or uncomfortable.

84
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:51,160
Exactly.

85
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:52,860
Uncomfortable, maybe controversial.

86
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:54,360
You worry about the reaction.

87
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:57,640
So it's just ignored, even though it's elephant-sized.

88
00:03:58,140 --> 00:03:59,600
The source examples were pretty clear.

89
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:02,490
During the meeting, no one addressed the elephant in the room.

90
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:04,670
The CEO's involvement in the scandal.

91
00:04:04,930 --> 00:04:05,070
Oof.

92
00:04:05,470 --> 00:04:05,650
Yeah.

93
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:09,260
Or maybe more direct, like, why isn't anyone talking about the elephant in the room?

94
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:10,200
He's incompetent.

95
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:11,760
It could be a big crisis, too.

96
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,800
Like, the financial crisis was the elephant in the room that no one wanted to discuss.

97
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:17,770
Until someone finally says,

98
00:04:18,070 --> 00:04:20,130
It's time to talk about the elephant in the room.

99
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:22,030
Forces the issue.

100
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:22,710
Right.

101
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:27,120
OK. Related to problems, sometimes your whole day feels like just reacting.

102
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,680
That's put out fires.

103
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:29,020
Right.

104
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,730
The source mentions put out, meaning extinguished.

105
00:04:31,770 --> 00:04:32,230
Precisely.

106
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:39,420
So metaphorically, you're spending your time dealing with one urgent crisis after another, extinguishing them as they pop up.

107
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:42,560
You hear people say, I can't get any real work done.

108
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:44,550
I spend most of my time putting out fires.

109
00:04:44,690 --> 00:04:47,810
Or maybe the IT department was busy putting out fires when the server crashed.

110
00:04:48,370 --> 00:04:50,590
It's very reactive, dealing with immediate problems.

111
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:54,880
And while you're busy with fires or elephants, it's easy to get lost in the weeds, right?

112
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:56,360
To go off on a tangent.

113
00:04:57,080 --> 00:04:59,540
The source calls this to go down the rabbit hole.

114
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:00,760
Yeah, from Alice in Wonderland.

115
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:05,890
It means you get sidetracked by interesting but ultimately distracting details.

116
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:07,830
You lose focus on the main thing.

117
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:09,950
Oh, happens constantly online.

118
00:05:10,350 --> 00:05:13,060
While researching, I went down a rabbit hole of related videos.

119
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:14,480
Definitely, or in a meeting.

120
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:18,740
We went down the rabbit hole discussing minor issues instead of the main agenda.

121
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:20,760
It's easy to do, and you just lose track of time.

122
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:26,730
Okay, but what about when you do address something perfectly, you solve the problem, or say exactly the right thing?

123
00:05:26,930 --> 00:05:28,990
That's to hit the nail on the head.

124
00:05:29,250 --> 00:05:29,450
Right.

125
00:05:29,790 --> 00:05:30,750
It means you're exactly right.

126
00:05:31,210 --> 00:05:37,300
Your analysis, your comment, your solution, it's perfectly accurate and relevant, spot on.

127
00:05:37,310 --> 00:05:41,140
Like your analysis of the market trends really hit the nail on the head.

128
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:42,480
Or describing someone.

129
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:44,380
You hit the nail on the head about him.

130
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:45,690
He is dishonest.

131
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:48,590
Even a doctor's diagnosis can hit the nail on the head.

132
00:05:49,130 --> 00:05:50,830
It just means perfect accuracy.

133
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:53,340
But sometimes, despite trying, you fall behind.

134
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:54,380
You're not keeping up.

135
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:57,580
That's when you find yourself having to play catch up.

136
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:05,390
Yeah, it means you're maybe behind competitors, behind schedule, behind on your workload, and you're making an effort to reach the level you should be at.

137
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:09,900
So after falling behind schedule, the team is now playing catch up to meet the deadline.

138
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:11,320
Or comparing companies.

139
00:06:12,210 --> 00:06:16,310
Our company is playing catch up with competitors who've already adopted new tech.

140
00:06:16,330 --> 00:06:18,370
It can feel like a constant struggle for some people.

141
00:06:19,630 --> 00:06:20,910
I always feel like I'm playing catch up.

142
00:06:21,190 --> 00:06:28,420
Okay, let's shift from problems to the work itself, effort, thoroughness, that kind of thing.

143
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:33,310
A common one for praising effort is to go the extra mile.

144
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:33,920
Right.

145
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:40,670
It means doing more than what's expected or required, putting in that additional effort, really trying hard.

146
00:06:40,890 --> 00:06:42,270
It's definitely seen as a good thing.

147
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:44,510
She always goes the extra mile for customers.

148
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:47,920
Or personally, he's always going the extra mile for his friends.

149
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:49,760
It often gets linked to success.

150
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:53,290
This project worked because the team was willing to go the extra mile.

151
00:06:53,270 --> 00:06:58,030
And for work that needs precision, we often hear to cross the T's and dot the I's.

152
00:06:58,210 --> 00:07:01,370
Comes right from the act of writing, making sure those little details are perfect.

153
00:07:01,670 --> 00:07:07,500
It means being meticulous, paying close attention to every single small detail, especially when finishing something.

154
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:09,540
The source mentioned legal contexts.

155
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:13,990
Before submitting the contract, the legal team crossed the T's and dotted the I's.

156
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:15,030
Makes sense there.

157
00:07:15,290 --> 00:07:15,770
Absolutely.

158
00:07:15,930 --> 00:07:17,530
Or describing someone.

159
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,070
She's known for her attention to detail.

160
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:22,520
Always takes the time to cross the T's and dot the I's in her reports.

161
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:24,700
It implies carefulness and accuracy.

162
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:28,870
And if you want to be really thorough, cover every possibility.

163
00:07:28,890 --> 00:07:32,150
You need to cover all the bases.

164
00:07:32,170 --> 00:07:34,950
Yeah, like in baseball, making sure every base is covered.

165
00:07:35,750 --> 00:07:41,720
It means considering every possible angle, doing everything necessary to make sure something is successful or complete.

166
00:07:42,370 --> 00:07:42,880
No gaps.

167
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:47,050
So, our procedures cover all the bases to ensure safety.

168
00:07:47,430 --> 00:07:47,790
Or...

169
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:52,620
The legal team reviewed the contract thoroughly to make sure it was covering all the bases.

170
00:07:53,490 --> 00:07:56,290
You might hear a manager check, are we covering all the bases here?

171
00:07:56,470 --> 00:07:58,170
Just making sure nothing's missed.

172
00:07:58,190 --> 00:08:02,700
And while you're covering bases, you want everyone working together, right, on the same wavelength.

173
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:04,240
That's to be on the same page.

174
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:04,900
Exactly.

175
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:10,830
It means everyone involved understands things the same way, agrees on the goals, or has the same information.

176
00:08:11,370 --> 00:08:12,400
Essential for teamwork.

177
00:08:12,550 --> 00:08:15,570
You might say, let's have a quick meeting to make sure everyone's on the same page.

178
00:08:15,630 --> 00:08:17,950
Or just ask directly, are we all on the same page?

179
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:23,360
And if not, you might point it out, hmm, I don't think we're on the same page regarding this topic.

180
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:25,180
It's all about shared understanding.

181
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:26,720
Okay, let's pivot a bit.

182
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:35,950
Let's talk about idioms related to strategy, creativity, risk, that side of business.

183
00:08:36,630 --> 00:08:40,210
A really popular one is to think outside the box.

184
00:08:40,350 --> 00:08:41,350
Ah, yes.

185
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:50,030
This means thinking creatively, originally, not being limited by the usual assumptions or methods, coming up with new unconventional ideas.

186
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:52,150
Which is highly valued in business, right?

187
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:52,950
For innovation.

188
00:08:53,110 --> 00:08:53,570
Definitely.

189
00:08:53,670 --> 00:08:57,280
So to solve this complex problem, you'll have to think outside the box.

190
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:00,580
Or instead of the usual approach, let's think outside the box.

191
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:03,090
People who do this are called out-of-the-box thinkers.

192
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:06,320
And when you think outside the box, you might create something that's cutting edge.

193
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:06,760
Right.

194
00:09:06,940 --> 00:09:11,950
That means the newest, most advanced, most innovative at the forefront of technology or development.

195
00:09:12,210 --> 00:09:14,580
Like this smartphone has a cutting edge design.

196
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:18,540
Or we need to stay updated on the latest cutting edge developments.

197
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:22,330
You could also say a company is on the cutting edge means they're leading the way.

198
00:09:22,470 --> 00:09:25,670
Now, strategically, sometimes the best move is the easiest one first.

199
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:30,020
That's called low hanging fruit or to pick low hanging fruit.

200
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:32,810
Yeah, literally the fruit that's easiest to reach on a tree.

201
00:09:33,370 --> 00:09:40,080
Metaphorically, it's the easiest tasks, the quickest wins, the opportunities that require the least effort for a decent return.

202
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:47,620
So to increase sales, let's focus on picking the low-hanging fruit by targeting existing customers, because that's easier than finding new ones.

203
00:09:47,870 --> 00:09:48,310
Exactly.

204
00:09:48,830 --> 00:09:54,990
Or for a project, let's pick the low-hanging fruit first to show some progress quickly before tackling the harder parts.

205
00:09:55,830 --> 00:09:57,600
It's about getting easy wins.

206
00:09:58,130 --> 00:10:03,700
But while picking easy fruit is okay sometimes, you generally don't want to put all your eggs in one basket.

207
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:04,800
Classic advice.

208
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:11,090
It means don't rely entirely on one single thing, one strategy, one investment, one client, one plan.

209
00:10:11,470 --> 00:10:13,950
Because if that one thing fails... You lose everything.

210
00:10:14,150 --> 00:10:15,410
Diversify your risk.

211
00:10:15,430 --> 00:10:15,890
Precisely.

212
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:17,420
Crucial in finance.

213
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:19,700
Don't put all your eggs in one basket when investing.

214
00:10:20,180 --> 00:10:21,520
Same for a job search.

215
00:10:21,540 --> 00:10:24,870
I'm applying for several jobs because I don't want to put all my eggs in one basket.

216
00:10:24,910 --> 00:10:30,500
And when you're thinking strategy, you need perspective, not just the details, but the overall view.

217
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:34,100
You need to see the big picture or to get the big picture.

218
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:34,780
Right.

219
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:43,170
It means understanding the whole situation, the overall context, the main goal, not just getting lost in the minor details or immediate tasks.

220
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:49,000
So someone might understand the steps, but he missed the big picture, meaning the overall purpose.

221
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:52,240
Or you might tell someone, stand back and take a look at the big picture.

222
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:53,620
Get some perspective.

223
00:10:53,650 --> 00:10:54,030
Okay.

224
00:10:54,090 --> 00:11:00,800
Moving towards, let's say, results, decisions, the nitty-gritty outcomes, everyone talks about the bottom line.

225
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:01,160
Yes.

226
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:02,460
Yeah.

227
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:03,180
This is fundamental.

228
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:08,030
It means the final result, the most important factor, the ultimate consequence.

229
00:11:08,690 --> 00:11:12,650
In business, it often literally means the net profit or loss.

230
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:14,560
But it's used more broadly, too, right?

231
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:19,030
Like the bottom line is he's gone, meaning that's the crucial final fact.

232
00:11:19,270 --> 00:11:19,650
Exactly.

233
00:11:20,150 --> 00:11:22,270
Or the bottom line is we don't have the money.

234
00:11:22,510 --> 00:11:23,620
That's the deciding factor.

235
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:28,600
In business strategy, let's not lose sight of the bottom line, which is increasing market share.

236
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:33,630
You also hear let's get to the bottom line, meaning get to the main point, cut the fluff.

237
00:11:33,610 --> 00:11:33,870
Right.

238
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:38,280
And speaking of getting to the point and making a decision, sometimes you just have to pull the trigger.

239
00:11:38,300 --> 00:11:40,260
It comes from firing a gun, obviously.

240
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:48,770
Metaphorically, it means making that final decision, committing to action, especially after hesitating or thinking for a long time.

241
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:53,560
You might hear someone impatiently say, you've been thinking about this for ages, just pull the trigger.

242
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:57,510
Or she finally pulled the trigger and quit her job, making that big move.

243
00:11:57,620 --> 00:11:58,280
In business.

244
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:01,330
It's time to pull the trigger on launching the new product.

245
00:12:02,030 --> 00:12:02,890
Stop deliberating.

246
